\id MRK Milestones — authored for this repository
\c 1
\p
\v 1 Milestones carry no text of their own, which is exactly why they are easy
to lose: a parser that drops them still produces the right words.
\v 2 \qt-s |sid="qt_1" who="Jesus"\*He said to them, "Follow me."\qt-e |eid="qt_1"\*
\v 3 A quotation milestone can span a verse boundary, so the start and end are
matched by their identifiers rather than by nesting. \qt-s |sid="qt_2" who="Jesus"\*The first
\v 4 half is here and the second half is in the next verse.\qt-e |eid="qt_2"\*
\p
\v 5 \ts-s\*A translator's section milestone takes no attributes at all and
self-closes immediately. \ts-e\*
\v 6 Word alignment writes the densest milestones in practice, one pair per
word: \zaln-s |x-strong="G09090" x-lemma="λόγος" x-morph="Gr,N,,,,,NMS,"\*\w Word|x-occurrence="1"\w*\zaln-e\* was with God.
\v 7 An unmatched start is a diagnostic, not a parse failure: \qt-s |sid="qt_3" who="Peter"\*
\p
\v 8 and the document continues past it.
